Verdict
==============================================================================
  !! SELF-DRAFT INVARIANT VIOLATED at batch 8: acceptance 0.9375 but the draft IS the target, so it must be exactly 1.0. The batched verify pass disagrees with the draft pass on identical weights and context -- treat as a numerical correctness bug.
  - Lossless at every batch size: False.
  - Self-draft acceptance exactly 1.0 at every batch size: False. Anything less is an engine bug, not a draft-quality result.
  - Batched verification saved verify passes in 5/5 multi-sequence configurations (a batch of one has nothing to batch).
  - Peak sequences verified per target pass: 8.0. A per-sequence verifier would have needed that many passes per round, so this is the factor by which speculation and batching compose rather than conflict.
  - Wall-clock speedup on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4050 Laptop GPU: 0.31x-1.62x.
  - Values below 1.0 are not a defect of the composition. The draft runs K passes serially per sequence per round, and this model pair (Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct against itself) is too close in cost for that to pay.
  - Note that this is *worse* on GPU than on CPU, which is the expected direction: a single target pass on a GPU is latency-bound rather than compute-bound, so it is already cheap, and the draft's serial passes dominate a larger share of the round. Speculation needs the target pass to be genuinely expensive before skipping some of them pays -- that means a much larger target, not merely a faster device.
  - So the plan's 2-3x claim is still neither confirmed nor refuted: it is about an 8B-class target, and this GPU has 6.0 GB of VRAM, which will not hold one in fp16.

